DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ssyyssrrcc utility retrieves rc.conf(5) variables from the collection of
     system rc files and allows processes with appropriate privilege to change
     values in a safe and effective manner.

     The following options are available:

     --aa       Dump a list of all non-default configuration variables.

     --AA       Dump a list of all configuration variables (incl. defaults).

     --cc       Check only.  For querying, return success if all requested vari‐
              ables are set (even if NULL), otherwise return error status.
              For assignments, return success if no changes are required, oth‐
              erwise failure.  If verbose (see “--vv”) prints a message stating
              whether variables are set and/or changes are required.

     --dd       Print a description of the given variable.

     --DD       Show default value(s) only (this is the same as setting RC_CONFS
              to NULL or passing `-f' with a NULL file-argument).

     --ee       Print query results as sh(1) compatible syntax (for example,
              ‘var=value’).  Ignored if either ‘--nn’ or ‘--FF’ is specified.

     --EE       When given ‘--ll’ or ‘--LL’ to list configuration files, only list
              those that exist.  When changing a setting, prefer to modify ex‐
              isting files.

     --ff _f_i_l_e  Operate on the specified file(s) instead of the files obtained
              by reading the ‘rc_conf_files’ entry in the RC_DEFAULTS file.
              This option can be specified multiple times for additional
              files.

     --FF       Show only the last rc.conf(5) file each directive is in.

     --hh       Print a short usage message to stderr and exit.

     ----hheellpp   Print a full usage statement to stderr and exit.

     --ii       Ignore unknown variables.

     --jj _j_a_i_l  The _j_i_d or name of the _j_a_i_l to operate within (overrides ‘--RR
              _d_i_r’; requires jexec(8)).

     --ll       List configuration files used at startup on stdout and exit.

     --LL       List all configuration files including rc.conf.d entries on std‐
              out and exit.  Can be combined with ‘--vv’ or ‘--ee’ to show service
              names.  ssyyssrrcc exits with success if all named services are in‐
              stalled, failure otherwise.

     --nn       Show only variable values, not their names.

     --NN       Show only variable names, not their values.

     --qq       Quiet.  Disable verbose and hide certain errors.  When combined
              with ‘--LL’ and one or more _n_a_m_e arguments, provide only exit sta‐
              tus and no output.

     --RR _d_i_r   Operate within the root directory ‘_d_i_r’ rather than ‘/’.

     --ss _n_a_m_e  If an rc.d script of _n_a_m_e exists (in “/etc/rc.d” or
              local_startup directories), process its “rc.conf.d” entries as
              potential overrides to ‘rc_conf_files’.  See rc.subr(8) for ad‐
              ditional information on “rc.conf.d”.  Can be combined with ‘--ll’
              to list configuration files used by service at startup.

     --vv       Verbose.  Print the pathname of the specific rc.conf(5) file
              where the directive was found.

     ----vveerrssiioonn
              Print version information to stdout and exit.

     --xx       Remove variable(s) from specified file(s).

     This utility has a similar syntax to sysctl(8).  It shares the `-e' and
     `-n' options (detailed above) and also has the same ‘name[=value]’ syntax
     for making queries/assignments.  In addition (but unlike sysctl(8)),
     ‘name+=value’ is supported for adding items to values (see APPENDING
     VALUES) and ‘name-=value’ is supported for removing items from values
     (see SUBTRACTING VALUES).

     However, while sysctl(8) serves to query/modify MIBs in the entrant ker‐
     nel, ssyyssrrcc instead works on values in the system rc.conf(5) configuration
     files.

     The list of system configuration files is configured in the file
     ‘/etc/defaults/rc.conf’ within the variable ‘rc_conf_files’, which by-de‐
     fault contains a space-separated list of pathnames.  On all FreeBSD sys‐
     tems, this defaults to the value "/etc/rc.conf /etc/rc.conf.local".  Each
     pathname is sourced in-order upon startup.  It is in the same fashion
     that ssyyssrrcc sources the configuration files before returning the value of
     the given variable.

     When supplied a variable name, ssyyssrrcc will return the value of the vari‐
     able.  If the variable does not appear in any of the configured
     ‘rc_conf_files’, an error is printed and error status is returned.

     When changing values of a given variable, it does not matter if the vari‐
     able appears in any of the ‘rc_conf_files’ or not.  If the variable does
     not appear in any of the files, it is appended to the end of the first
     pathname in the ‘rc_conf_files’ variable.  Otherwise, ssyyssrrcc will replace
     only the last-occurrence in the last-file found to contain the variable.
     This gets the value to take effect next boot without heavily modifying
     these integral files (yet taking care not to allow the file to grow un‐
     wieldy should ssyyssrrcc be called repeatedly).

AAPPPPEENNDDIINNGG VVAALLUUEESS
     When using the ‘key+=value’ syntax to add items to existing values, the
     first character of the value is taken as the delimiter separating items
     (usually " " or ",").  For example, in the following statement:

           ssyyssrrcc cloned_interfaces+=" gif0"

     the first character is a space, informing ssyyssrrcc that existing values are
     to be considered separated by whitespace.  If ‘gif0’ is not found in the
     existing value for _c_l_o_n_e_d___i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e_s, it is added (with delimiter only if
     existing value is non-NULL).

     For convenience, if the first character is alpha-numeric (letters A-Z,
     a-z, or numbers 0-9), dot (.), or slash (/), ssyyssrrcc uses the default set‐
     ting of whitespace as separator.  For example, the above and below state‐
     ments are equivalent since “gif0” starts with an alpha-numeric character
     (the letter g):

           ssyyssrrcc cloned_interfaces+=gif0

     Take the following sequence for example:

           ssyyssrrcc cloned_interfaces= # start with NULL

           ssyyssrrcc cloned_interfaces+=gif0
                 # NULL -> `gif0' (NB: no preceding delimiter)

           ssyyssrrcc cloned_interfaces+=gif0 # no change

           ssyyssrrcc cloned_interfaces+="tun0 gif0"
                 # `gif0' -> `gif0 tun0' (NB: no duplication)

     ssyyssrrcc prevents the same value from being added if already there.

SSUUBBTTRRAACCTTIINNGG VVAALLUUEESS
     When using the ‘key-=value’ syntax to remove items from existing values,
     the first character of the value is taken as the delimiter separating
     items (usually " " or ",").  For example, in the following statement:

           cclloonneedd__iinntteerrffaacceess--=="" ggiiff00""

     the first character is a space, informing ssyyssrrcc that existing values are
     to be considered separated by whitespace.  If ‘gif0’ is found in the ex‐
     isting value for _c_l_o_n_e_d___i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e_s, it is removed (extra delimiters
     removed).

     For convenience, if the first character is alpha-numeric (letters A-Z,
     a-z, or numbers 0-9), dot (.), or slash (/), ssyyssrrcc uses the default set‐
     ting of whitespace as separator.  For example, the above and below state‐
     ments are equivalent since “gif0” starts with an alpha-numeric character
     (the letter g):

           ssyyssrrcc cloned_interfaces-=gif0

     Take the following sequence for example:

           ssyyssrrcc foo="bar baz" # start

           ssyyssrrcc foo-=bar # `bar baz' -> `baz'

           ssyyssrrcc foo-=baz # `baz' -> NULL

     ssyyssrrcc removes all occurrences of all items provided and collapses extra
     delimiters between items.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     The following environment variables are referenced by ssyyssrrcc:

     RC_CONFS     Override default ‘rc_conf_files’ (even if set to NULL).

     RC_DEFAULTS  Location of ‘/etc/defaults/rc.conf’ file.

DDEEPPEENNDDEENNCCIIEESS
     The following standard commands are required by ssyyssrrcc:

     awk(1), cat(1), chmod(1), env(1), grep(1), mktemp(1), mv(1), rm(1),
     sh(1), stat(1), tail(1), chown(8), chroot(8), jls(8), and jexec(8).

